    {
      "id": "uejjeu0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.2 Physical Divisions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "okz6buj",
      "content": "The state can be divided into three broad physical zones from south to north:"
    },
    {
      "id": "m85iseg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "(a) The Outer Himalayan Zone (Foothills / Siwalik Range)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8w5izn1",
      "content": "The southern fringe of Arunachal Pradesh consists of low hills and plains transitioning from the Brahmaputra floodplains of Assam. This zone, at elevations of 100–1,000 metres, is characterised by dense tropical forests, river valleys, and relatively flat terrain. Districts such as Papum Pare, Lohit, and Namsai have significant areas in this zone."
    },
    {
      "id": "2qtcs42",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "(b) The Middle Himalayan Zone"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ograxi2",
      "content": "This zone, at elevations of 1,000–3,000 metres, consists of parallel hill ranges covered by temperate forests. Several major rivers cut through this zone forming deep gorges and valleys. Tribes such as the Adi, Galo, and Apatani inhabit this region."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The Trans-Himalayan Zone (Greater Himalayas)",
      "id": "n30ak2k"
    },
    {
      "id": "r7ufd89",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "(c) The Trans-Himalayan Zone (Greater Himalayas)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wi1f6sr",
      "content": "The northernmost zone consists of the Greater Himalayas, with elevations exceeding 3,000 metres and reaching up to 7,090 metres. The terrain includes alpine meadows, permanent snowfields, and glaciers. The Tawang district, famous for its Buddhist monastery, falls largely within this zone."
    },
    {
      "id": "zryt0za",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.3 Major Rivers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ze8uz8n",
      "content": "Arunachal Pradesh is one of the richest states in river systems. Most rivers originate in the Himalayas or Tibet and flow southward to join the Brahmaputra in Assam."
    },
